  "user_scenario": "You are Renata Kowalski, the payroll administrator of Larkspur Table Group LLC, which runs three restaurants in Columbus, Ohio. It is Friday morning, a few hours before the weekend tip-outs start, and you are calling Paystride's employer desk from the back office with your laptop open. Your servers' tips go out through the company's Paystride Instant Pay service, and the balance keeps running thin before weekends. You want to put money in yourself today from Larkspur's operating account by ACH, enough to get the account properly covered, and you need the desk to tell you exactly how much to send. You open with that: you'd like to send an ACH credit today so the Instant Pay account is properly covered, and you need the exact amount.\n\nWhen the agent asks who you are, you give the business name, Larkspur Table Group LLC, and say you are Renata Kowalski, the payroll administrator on the account. For verification you give the phone number on file, 6465550176, and your work email, rkowalski@larkspurtable.com; if one more detail is wanted you can give the billing ZIP code, 43215, or the last four digits of the company's EIN, 3926. You don't keep Paystride reference numbers and leave all lookups to the agent. Only if the agent asks whether Larkspur uses other Paystride programs do you say that your staff also have Paystride Earned Wage Access through the company; you don't know its settings. Only if the agent asks about earlier deposits do you mention that your bank pulled back a payment you had accidentally sent twice in March.\n\nYou don't know Paystride's levels or formulas and you never suggest a figure. If the agent explains that the account's low-balance alert level has to be confirmed at a new amount, you agree, and when the agent hands you that confirmation you run it yourself straight away with exactly the amount the agent gives you. If the agent tells you a monthly fee is being taken today or that an automatic top-up will be pulled from your bank under your existing authorization, you say that's fine. When the agent gives you the amount to send and hands you the ACH credit, you send it yourself with exactly that amount; if the agent asks you to send money without giving an amount, you ask how much first. Once the agent tells you the credit has been booked, you thank them and end the call.",
